 headline:"Look closely at the cup",
 alt:"A wide silver cup with two handles, seen from a slight angle, standing on a round, flaring foot. A band of gold-colored woven patterns circles the bowl just below the rim, dotted with round blue and red studs. Beside a handle, three larger studs sit on a cloverleaf-shaped panel. The rest of the bowl is smooth, plain silver with faint engraved letters.",
 enlarge:"Enlarge image",
 image:"public/images/artworks/Ardagh-Chalice.jpg",
 imageNote:"A careful copy of the Ardagh Chalice, made in the early 1900s. The original was made in Ireland about 750 C.E.",
 detail:{image:"public/images/artworks/Ardagh-Chalice-Detail.jpg",alt:"A close view of one handle and the panels around it. Small squares of blue and red enamel are set in rows on the handle. Next to it, three round studs in blue, red and green sit on a cloverleaf panel packed with tiny woven wire patterns.",caption:"Technique detail: on this copy, fine twisted wires form woven panels and colored enamel studs sit beside them, as on the original",credit:{word:"Detail photo",url:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ardagh_Chalice_MET_tem07658s2.jpg",author:"The Metropolitan Museum of Art (Rogers Fund, 1906)",license:"CC0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/",changes:"Cropped from the original."}},
 size:"This copy: about 16 cm / 6\u00bc in high and 19 cm / 7\u00bd in wide. About the size of a large soup bowl.",
 intro:"DESCRIPTION ONLY \u2014 exact text not in revision record. Record says: Design-strategy question: asks students to look at where filigree bands, enamel studs, and plain metal are placed, find the pattern in how decorated and undecorated space is divided, and consider what effect that contrast creates.",
 context:[{term:"Filigree",pron:"FIL-ih-gree",def:"decoration made from fine threads of gold or silver wire, twisted, coiled, and soldered to a metal surface. The Ardagh Chalice's filigree panels are so fine the patterns are nearly invisible to the naked eye."},{term:"Enamel",pron:"ih-NAM-ul",def:"a glassy coating made by fusing powdered minerals onto metal with heat, adding permanent color to metalwork. Related to cloisonn\u00e9, a technique in which thin metal walls hold the enamel in separate cells."}],
