var EM20={id:"EM-20",kind:"Burial artifact",title:"Red Stones in Gold",object:"Sutton Hoo purse lid",playable:true,
 crumb:"Early Medieval Period, circa 300 C.E. \u2013 900 C.E.",
 work:"Sutton Hoo purse lid",date:"early 7th century C.E.",location:"British Museum, London (found at Sutton Hoo, Suffolk, England)",artist:"unknown Anglo-Saxon craftsman",
 headline:"Look closely at the lid",
 alt:"A flat gold lid with a gently curved lower edge, mounted on a pale backing, with three hinges along the top. A border of tiny red cells alternating with blue-and-white checks runs around the edge. In the middle, two curved birds with hooked beaks face each other, each gripping a smaller bird. On either side of them, a figure with a face and arms stands between two curled beasts. Above these, two six-sided panels of fine gold patterns flank a panel of interlaced animals.",
 enlarge:"Enlarge image",
 image:"public/images/artworks/SuttonHoo-PurseLid.jpg",
 imageNote:"The gold lid of a purse buried in a ship burial in England about 1,400 years ago. The pale backing is a modern display mount.",
 detail:{image:"public/images/artworks/SuttonHoo-PurseLid-Detail.jpg",alt:"A close view of the two central panels. Each shows a bird of prey with a hooked beak and a round blue-and-white eye, gripping a smaller bird in its claws. The red bodies are set in thin gold walls, and tiny gold feathers fill the gaps.",caption:"Technique detail: two birds built from red stones and glass set inside thin gold walls",credit:{word:"Detail photo",url:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutton_hoo_purse_lid02017_(bird_of_pray).JPG",author:"Geni",license:"CC BY-SA 4.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/",changes:"Used as provided."}},
 size:"About 19 cm / 7\u00bd in long and 8.3 cm / 3\u00bc in tall, not counting the hinges. About as long as a new pencil.",
 intro:"This gold purse lid was buried in an Anglo-Saxon ship grave at Sutton Hoo, England, around 625 C.E. The surface is covered in cloisonné — thin gold walls forming tiny cells that hold precisely cut garnets and colored glass. Among the geometric patterns, the craftsman set zoomorphic panels: birds of prey gripping smaller birds, and a man standing between two beasts. Look at how the same designs repeat on the left and right sides. Why might the craftsman have made both halves mirror each other so exactly?",
 context:[{term:"Cloisonn\u00e9",pron:"klwah-zuh-NAY",def:"a metalwork technique in which thin strips of gold are soldered to a surface to form tiny cells, each filled with a precisely cut piece of garnet, colored glass, or enamel. The gold walls catch the light between the colored stones."},{term:"Zoomorphic",pron:"zoh-uh-MOR-fik",def:"using animal forms as decoration. On this lid, the birds of prey and the beasts flanking the central figure are zoomorphic panels \u2014 stylized animals arranged into a decorative design."}],
 clues:[{id:"garnets",label:"Cloisonné garnets",def:"Precisely cut pieces of deep red garnet set into tiny gold cells, with the gold walls catching light between the stones"},{id:"birds",label:"Zoomorphic birds",def:"Curved birds of prey with hooked beaks, each gripping a smaller bird in its talons, mirrored on both sides of the lid"},{id:"beasts",label:"Man between beasts",def:"A standing human figure flanked by two animals, a composition found across many cultures from ancient Mesopotamia to early medieval Europe"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.",{head:"Choose two.",body:"Write at least one sentence about the feature. Try to use a Word to Know."}],
 closing:"Why might a craftsman have put so much gold and stone into the lid of a small purse?",
 bonus:{points:5,prompt:"For 5 bonus points, add any additional observations about the work in general, or question(s) you would like to pursue later.",placeholder:""},
 guide:"Evidence filed. Red stone inlay, birds of prey and a figure between beasts are features a detective can carry to the next case.",
 sourceNums:[48,49],
 credit:{word:"Photo",url:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutton_hoo_(1).JPG",author:"Jononmac46",license:"CC BY-SA 3.0",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/",changes:"Resized from the original."}};
