 imageNote:"",
 detail:{image:"public/images/artworks/Luttrell-Servant.jpg",alt:"A bearded servant in a long yellow tunic with a belt and a small pouch. He leans forward and holds out two dark bowls with both hands. He wears pointed black shoes and thin pinkish leggings. At the top right, part of a diner in a dark hood sits behind the table.",caption:"Technique detail: up close, the servant is drawn with thin dark outlines and flat patches of color",credit:{word:"Detail image",url:"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luttrell_servant.jpg",author:"Unknown artists (Luttrell Psalter, British Library Add MS 42130)",license:"Public domain",licenseUrl:"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/",changes:"Shown at its original size."}},
 size:"",
 intro:"Almost 700 years ago, a wealthy English landowner paid a team of artists to decorate his personal book of psalms. The painters crowded the margins with scenes from daily life: ploughing, baking, carrying food. Why might the painters have filled the edges of a book of prayers with ordinary workers?",
 context:[{term:"Psalter",pron:"",def:"a handwritten book of psalms, the songs and prayers of the Bible."},{term:"Marginalia",pron:"mar-jin-AY-lee-uh",def:"small pictures painted in the margins of a page, around the main text or scene."},{term:"Illumination",pron:"",def:"a page decorated with painted scenes, patterned borders and, often, gold leaf that catches the light."}],
 clues:[{id:"panel",label:"Diapered panel",def:"A rectangle of dark blue dotted with white, closed in by an orange border"},{id:"work",label:"Figures in the margin",def:"Servants doing ordinary jobs, such as carrying bowls and serving a meal, while they stand outside the panel"},{id:"vine",label:"Vine border",def:"A blue stem that loops and curls, with orange and green leaves, running down the side of the page"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.","Choose any two features to write about. For each, write at least one sentence about your own observations or what you find most interesting."],
 closing:"The diners sit behind the table, but the dishes look as if they are seen from above. Why might the artist have drawn the table top this way?",
