 size:"",
 intro:"Almost 800 years ago, a sculptor carved this life-size stone statue of a noblewoman named Uta. She had died close to 200 years before he began. Why would a sculptor make a statue of someone no one still alive had ever seen?",
 context:[{term:"Polychrome",pron:"POL-ee-krome",def:"painted in many colors. Traces of the original paint survive on Uta."},{term:"Founder figure",pron:"",def:"a carved statue of a person who gave money or land to help build a church, placed inside the building to honor them."},{term:"Naturalism",pron:"",def:"a style that aims to show people and things as they actually look, instead of in a stiff or formal way."}],
 clues:[{id:"face",label:"An individual face",def:"A face carved to look like one particular person, with its own eyes, mouth and expression"},{id:"hand",label:"A natural gesture",def:"A natural gesture, with one hand raising the edge of a cloak up beside the face"},{id:"crown",label:"Surviving paint",def:"A crown covered in gold paint, with small leaf-like shapes along its top edge"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.","Choose any two features to write about. For each, write at least one sentence about your own observations or what you find most interesting."],
 closing:"Compare Uta with the stretched figures at Autun, made about a century earlier. How does each artist make a figure look like a particular person, or not?",
