 size:"",
 intro:"About 900 years ago, painters in this Romanesque abbey church covered a long stone ceiling with scenes from the Bible. Instead of one huge picture, they divided the ceiling into rows, each holding several scenes. Why might the painters have divided the story into rows instead of painting one large scene?",
 context:[{term:"Romanesque",pron:"roh-muh-NESK",def:"a medieval building style from about 900 to 1200. Thick walls, round arches and heavy stone give it a solid, massive feel, like Roman buildings before it."},{term:"Vault",pron:"VAWLT",def:"a ceiling built as a curved arch. Here, one long, rounded vault runs the length of the church."},{term:"Register",pron:"",def:"a horizontal band holding a row of scenes or figures. Several registers can be stacked one above another."},{term:"Fresco",pron:"",def:"a wall painting made by applying paint to fresh, wet plaster so the colors dry into the wall."}],
