var LM25={id:"LM-25",kind:"Ivory carving",title:"A Tilted Belt, Cloth Falling Across and a Leaning Body",object:"Virgin and Child from the Sainte-Chapelle",playable:true,
 crumb:"Late Medieval Period, circa 900 C.E. \u2013 1400 C.E.",
 work:"Virgin and Child from the Sainte-Chapelle",date:"c. 1260\u20131279 C.E.",location:"Mus\u00e9e du Louvre, Paris",artist:"Carver unknown",
 headline:"Look closely at the way she stands",
 alt:"A small standing statue carved in cream-white ivory, shown in a museum case. A woman in a long robe and cloak holds a child on her left arm. She wears a rounded cap with a veil falling beside her face, and she turns her head slightly toward the child, who sits on her raised arm and reaches a hand toward her shoulder. A narrow gold belt crosses her waist on a slant, rising toward the child's side instead of running level, and below it her body swings out that way. From that side the cloak falls in long curved folds that sweep down and across the front of the robe toward the opposite side, while on that other side the cloth hangs in a plain, almost straight line to the floor. Thin gold borders and lines of gold pattern run along the hems and the neckline, and a small dark red stone is set at her breast. She stands on a shallow many-sided base carved with a row of diamond shapes. Her head does not sit above the middle of that base.",
 enlarge:"Enlarge image",
 image:"public/images/artworks/SainteChapelle-Virgin.jpg",
 imageNote:"The statuette is photographed in its case in the Louvre. The blurred red and brown shapes behind it are the gallery and a display panel.",
 detail:{image:"public/images/artworks/SainteChapelle-Virgin-Detail.jpg",alt:"A close view of the upper part of the same statue. The woman's face is seen nearly in profile, her eyes lowered toward the child. She wears a rounded cap over a veil with a decorated edge. The child sits on her arm, smiling, holding a round fruit in one hand and reaching the other up to touch her shoulder. Gold patterns run along her neckline, down the front edge of her cloak, and around the child's collar and cuffs. A dark red stone is set at her breast and another at the child's. Fine cracks run through the ivory.",caption:"Technique detail: gold patterning along the hems, small set stones, and the look exchanged between mother and child. Photographed in 2018.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vierge_%C3%A0_l%27Enfant_de_la_Sainte-Chapelle_(Louvre,_OA_57)_detail2.jpg", "author": "Tangopaso", "license": "Public domain", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}},
 size:"Height: 41 cm / 16 in. Small enough to hold in two hands.",
 intro:"Nothing on this little figure stands straight up. Can you find three features?",
 context:"An S-curve is the shallow S shape a standing figure makes when the body bends one way at the hips and the other way at the shoulders, instead of standing straight.",
 clues:[{"id": "belt", "label": "A belt on a slant", "def": "The narrow gold belt crosses her waist on a slant instead of running level, because the hips beneath it are not level"}, {"id": "folds", "label": "Cloth falling across", "def": "Long folds of the cloak pour diagonally from the high side of the belt, down and across the front of the robe"}, {"id": "tilt", "label": "A leaning body", "def": "Her head does not sit above the middle of the base; the whole figure leans"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.", "Choose any two features to write about. For each, write at least one sentence about your own observations or what you find most interesting."],
 closing:"Compare her with the standing statues at Reims and with the Gero Cross. The Gero figure hangs heavy and straight. The Reims figures and this one bend. Now think about the material. This whole statue was cut from a single elephant tusk, and a tusk is curved; the Louvre says the figure keeps that curve. Which do you think came first here \u2014 the carver's idea of how a body should stand, or the shape of the material he had to work with?",
 bonus:{points:5,prompt:"For 5 bonus points, add any additional observations about the work in general, or question(s) you would like to pursue later.",placeholder:""},
 guide:"Evidence filed. A tilted belt, cloth falling across the body and a leaning line are features a detective can carry to the next case.",
 sourceNums:[126,127],
 credit:{"word": "Photo", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louvre_Virgen_Sainte_Chapelle.JPG", "author": "Miguel Hermoso Cuesta", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}};
