var LM27={id:"LM-27",kind:"Stone sculpture",title:"A Crown, a Carved Harness and a Perch on the Wall",object:"The Bamberg Rider",playable:true,
 crumb:"Late Medieval Period, circa 900 C.E. \u2013 1400 C.E.",
 work:"The Bamberg Rider",date:"c. 1225\u20131237 C.E.",location:"Bamberg Cathedral, Bavaria, Germany",artist:"Sculptor unknown",
 headline:"Look closely at the rider and at where he stands",
 alt:"A life-size stone statue of a young man on horseback, set against the pillar of a church. The horse stands facing right, its head turned slightly toward the viewer, with a braided mane, a bridle of carved straps and a chain at the bit. The rider sits upright. He wears a crown over long wavy hair, a tunic and a cloak fastened at the shoulder, and he carries no sword, shield or armor. He holds the reins in one hand and the cord of his cloak in the other, and his head is turned away from the way the horse is walking, so that he looks out to one side. Above him, jutting from the pillar, hangs a small canopy carved as a stone building with gables, pointed arches and pinnacles. Below, the horse's hooves rest on a bracket carved with deep curling leaves, which projects from the pillar well above the floor. The stone is pale and bare.",
 enlarge:"Enlarge image",
 image:"public/images/artworks/Bamberg-Rider.jpg",
 imageNote:"The statue stands against the north pillar of the Saint George choir. It is carved from reed sandstone, and traces of color show that it was once painted.",
 detail:{flex:55,image:"public/images/artworks/Bamberg-Rider-Detail.jpg",alt:"A close view of the horse's head and neck. The mane is carved as a row of tight braided curls along the crest. A bridle of narrow carved straps, marked with rows of small notches, runs over the brow and down the cheek, and a metal chain hangs from the bit at the mouth. The eye, the nostril and the bones of the face are carved with care. Behind, the arches and piers of the church are out of focus.",caption:"Technique detail: the braided mane and the bridle, carved strap by strap. The chain at the bit is metal.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo (horse)", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bamberg-Dom-Bamberger_Reiter-16-Pferd-2009-gje.jpg", "author": "Gerd Eichmann", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}},
 detail2:{image:"public/images/artworks/Bamberg-Rider-Detail2.jpg",flex:42,alt:"A close view of the rider's head and shoulders. He wears a crown, a broad band studded with raised bosses and topped with small carved leaves. His hair falls in long carved waves on either side of his face. The brow is furrowed, the eyes are open and turned to one side, and the mouth is closed. At his shoulder a cord crosses his chest, and he holds its carved clasp between the fingers of one hand. Behind him the tracery of the stone canopy is out of focus; at the right edge is the horse's ear and mane.",caption:"Technique detail: the crown, the carved waves of hair, and the eyes turned away from the way the horse is walking.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo (face)", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Detail_Bamberger_Reiter.jpg", "author": "Holger Uwe Schmitt", "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/", "changes": "Cropped and resized from the original."}},
 size:"The horse is life-size, and the group stands high on a pillar, above the heads of everyone in the church.",
 intro:"Almost 800 years ago, a sculptor carved a king on horseback and attached him, life-size, to a pillar inside this German cathedral. He carries no weapon, no shield and no banner. Scholars have argued for centuries about who he is. Why would an artist leave out the clues that would identify a famous person?",
 context:[{term:"Equestrian",pron:"eh-KWES-tree-un",def:"a statue or picture of a person riding a horse."},{term:"Attribute",pron:"",def:"an object a figure holds or wears that tells you who he or she is, such as a crown for a king or a sword for a knight."},{term:"Canopy",pron:"",def:"a small roof carved over a statue, like a tiny architectural shelter."}],
 clues:[{"id": "crown", "label": "A crown but no attributes", "def": "A crown is an attribute of a king. What other attributes would tell you MORE about this figure? What is missing?"}, {"id": "harness", "label": "A real harness", "def": "Look at how carefully the straps, bridle and braided mane are carved. What does this tell you about the sculptor's method?"}, {"id": "perch", "label": "High on the pillar", "def": "The statue sits on a leafy bracket under a canopy, well above the heads of the people in the church. What does this placement say about who he is?"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.", "Choose any two features to write about. For each, write at least one sentence about your own observations or what you find most interesting."],
 closing:"Two things about this case are still open. The stone looks bare, but traces of color survive: the horse was white, the robe red with gold, the crown gold, the base green. And nobody knows who he is. Because he wears a royal crown and carries no weapon, Bamberg's own guide calls Saint Stephen of Hungary the likeliest candidate and rules out Emperor Henry II, who is buried in this cathedral, since an emperor would wear an imperial crown. What evidence would you need to settle it?",
 bonus:{points:5,prompt:"For 5 bonus points, add any additional observations about the work in general, or question(s) you would like to pursue later.",placeholder:""},
 guide:"Evidence filed. A crown, a carved harness and a perch on the wall are features a detective can carry to the next case.",
 sourceNums:[130,131],
 credit:{"word": "Photo", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2020-07-03_07-05_Franken_102_Bamberg,_Dom,_Bamberger_Reiter_(50126573137).jpg", "author": "Allie Caulfield", "license": "CC BY 2.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}};
