var LM28={id:"LM-28",kind:"Panel painting",wide:true,title:"Raised Gold Words, an Olive Branch and a Figure Drawing Back",object:"The Annunciation, from Siena Cathedral",playable:true,
 crumb:"Late Medieval Period, circa 900 C.E. \u2013 1400 C.E.",
 work:"Annunciation with Saint Ansanus",date:"1333 C.E.",location:"Galleria degli Uffizi, Florence",artist:"Simone Martini and Lippo Memmi",
 headline:"Look closely at what passes between the two figures",
 alt:"A large altarpiece of three painted panels in a tall gold frame with pointed gables, spires and four round portraits of prophets along the top. The background of every panel is plain gold. In the wide centre panel an angel kneels at the left, in a white and gold robe, with great patterned wings still spread and his cloak swirling behind him as though he has just landed. He wears a wreath of dark olive leaves with a small crown, and holds a branch of olive in his lower hand. His mouth is open, and a line of gold letters rises from it across the gold background toward the woman opposite. Between them a slim vase holds white lilies. At the right a woman in a deep blue cloak sits on an inlaid throne. She presses back against it and pulls the cloak across her body, one hand at her throat, her thumb still holding her place in a red book. Above the centre a white dove flies down in a ring of winged heads. A young saint with a banner stands in the left panel and a veiled saint with a cross and a palm in the right.",
 enlarge:"Enlarge image",
 image:"public/images/artworks/Martini-Annunciation.jpg",
 imageNote:"Painted for the altar of Saint Ansanus in Siena Cathedral and moved to Florence in 1799. Ansanus, patron saint of Siena, stands at the left with the city's banner; the saint at the right has never been securely identified. The gold frame is a 19th-century replacement, with a fragment of the original frame set into it carrying the two painters' names and the year 1333.",
 detail:{flex:58,image:"public/images/artworks/Martini-Annunciation-Detail.jpg",alt:"A close view of the centre of the painting. At the left the angel's head is seen in profile, wearing a wreath of dark olive leaves set with a small jewelled crown, his mouth open and a thin gold halo behind him. From his mouth a line of Latin capitals rises across the plain gold background, the letters standing up from the surface and catching the light. At the right edge the words reach the face of the woman, under the tooled rays of her halo. Below, a dark olive branch and the pale trumpets of lilies cross the gold.",caption:"Technique detail: the angel's greeting, AVE GRATIA PLENA DOMINVS TECVM, built up in relief on the gold so that the letters catch the light.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo (the greeting)", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone_Martini_%E2%80%94_Annunciation_with_St._Margaret_and_St._Ansanus.jpg", "author": "Gleb Simonov", "license": "CC0 1.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/", "changes": "Cropped and resized from the original."}},
 detail2:{flex:38,image:"public/images/artworks/Martini-Annunciation-Detail2.jpg",alt:"A close view of the right side of the painting. A woman in a deep blue cloak with a gold-bordered edge sits on a wooden throne inlaid with small patterns. She leans back and twists away, her knees turned one way and her shoulders the other, drawing the cloak across her body. One hand is raised to her throat. In the other she holds a red book, her thumb still between the pages. Behind her head a gold halo is tooled with fine rays and a ring of flowers. The end of the gold lettering runs across the background at the upper left, above a stem of lilies.",caption:"Technique detail: Mary pressing back into the throne, her thumb still marking her place in the book.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo (Mary)", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone_Martini_%E2%80%94_Annunciation_with_St._Margaret_and_St._Ansanus.jpg", "author": "Gleb Simonov", "license": "CC0 1.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/", "changes": "Cropped and resized from the original."}},
 size:"The three panels together are about 305 cm / 10 ft wide and 265 cm / 8 ft 8 in high. The two painters signed the work in Latin, with the year.",
 intro:"An angel has just landed, and the girl he came to speak to is pulling away from him. Can you find three features?",
 context:"A gold ground is a background covered in gold leaf instead of a painted sky or room.",
 clues:[{"id": "words", "label": "Words written in gold", "def": "A line of Latin capitals crossing the gold background from the angel's mouth toward the woman, raised up from the surface"}, {"id": "olive", "label": "An olive branch", "def": "A wreath of olive leaves on the angel's head, and a branch of olive held in his lower hand"}, {"id": "recoil", "label": "A figure drawing back", "def": "The woman pressing back into her throne and pulling her cloak across her body, her thumb still holding her place in a book"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.", "Choose any two features to write about. For each, write at least one sentence about your own observations or what you find most interesting."],
 closing:"Set this beside Giotto's Lamentation, painted in the same years. Giotto gives his figures weight, a stony hillside and real ground to stand on. Simone Martini gives his a floor of marble, robes of watered silk \u2014 and a sky of solid gold, with words laid across it that you could run a finger over. Both painters were after something true. Which of the two do you think a person kneeling at this altar in 1333 would have found more convincing, and why?",
 bonus:{points:5,prompt:"For 5 bonus points, add any additional observations about the work in general, or question(s) you would like to pursue later.",placeholder:""},
 guide:"Evidence filed. Raised gold words, an olive branch and a figure drawing back are features a detective can carry to the next case.",
 sourceNums:[132,133],
 credit:{"word": "Photo", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simone_Martini_%E2%80%94_Annunciation_with_St._Margaret_and_St._Ansanus.jpg", "author": "Gleb Simonov", "license": "CC0 1.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}};
