var LM29={id:"LM-29",kind:"Tapestry",wide:true,title:"A Giant Reader, Two Rows of Scenes and Backgrounds That Switch",object:"The Apocalypse Tapestry",playable:true,
 crumb:"Late Medieval Period, circa 900 C.E. \u2013 1400 C.E.",
 work:"The Apocalypse Tapestry",date:"1377\u20131382 C.E.",location:"Ch\u00e2teau d\u2019Angers, France",artist:"Designed by Jean Bondol; woven in Paris",
 headline:"Look closely at how the story is laid out",
 alt:"A very long woven hanging displayed along a dark gallery wall, seen at an angle so that it runs away into the distance. At the left a huge seated figure in a red robe sits under a tall carved canopy, holding an open scroll; he is several times the size of the figures beside him. To his right the hanging is divided into framed scenes stacked in two rows, one above the other, which continue down the wall as far as the picture goes. The background behind the figures in each scene is either deep red or dark blue scattered with small flowers, and it changes from one to the other from scene to scene. A pale strip of sky runs above the upper row and a strip of flowered ground below the lower row. The wool has faded to soft browns, greens and pale blues.",
 enlarge:"Enlarge image",
 image:"public/images/artworks/Angers-Apocalypse.jpg",
 imageNote:"Louis I, Duke of Anjou, ordered the set in about 1373, and it was woven in Paris between 1377 and 1382 from designs by Jean Bondol, who worked for the king. The colors have faded badly on the front. The backs of the panels still hold the deep reds and blues the weavers put there.",
 detail:{flex:52,image:"public/images/artworks/Angers-Apocalypse-Detail.jpg",alt:"A close view of one scene. At the left a winged angel in a white robe stands blowing a long horn, with a golden halo and great feathered wings. From the mouth of the horn a shower of thin red lines falls through a dark blue sky, past a white star, down into a pale sea below. At the centre and right a group of people recoil, one throwing up his hands, another bending away. At the far right a bearded man in a red cloak stands inside a small arched building, holding a book and watching. The weave of the wool is visible across the whole surface.",caption:"Technique detail: an angel sounds the third trumpet and a burning star falls. The man watching from the little building at the right is John, who is said to have seen all this.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo (the third trumpet)", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France-001405_-_Apocalypse_Tapestry_(15372643572).jpg", "author": "Dennis G. Jarvis", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}},
 detail2:{flex:44,image:"public/images/artworks/Angers-Apocalypse-Detail2.jpg",alt:"A close view of another scene. A bearded man in a red cloak stands praying inside a small arched building at the left. Beside him a walled city collapses into rubble, its towers and roofs falling sideways. Winged demons and birds tumble through a dark blue background covered in a pattern of curling stems and red flowers. At the right two small groups of people stand on a pale green hillside, watching and turning away. The edges of the hanging are worn and pale.",caption:"Technique detail: the fall of the city, woven against a blue ground patterned with stems and flowers.",credit:{"word": "Detail photo (the fall of Babylon)", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Angers-_Apocalypse_(11).JPG", "author": "R\u00e9mi Jouan", "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}},
 size:"Originally about 140 m / 460 ft long and 6 m / 20 ft high \u2014 some 840 square metres of wool, longer than a football field. Ninety scenes were woven and about seventy survive. UNESCO added it to its Memory of the World register in 2023.",
 intro:"This is one wall of a picture that was once longer than a football field. Can you find three features?",
 context:"A tapestry is a picture woven into the cloth itself, on a loom, as the cloth is being made. It is not sewn on afterwards.",
 clues:[{"id": "reader", "label": "A giant seated reader", "def": "One huge figure, far larger than anyone in the scenes, seated under a carved canopy with an open scroll"}, {"id": "rows", "label": "Two rows of scenes", "def": "Framed scenes stacked in two rows, one above the other, running away down the wall"}, {"id": "switch", "label": "Backgrounds that switch", "def": "The background behind the figures changing from red to blue and back again, scene by scene"}],
 steps:["Click a feature box below, then click where you identify the feature on the image. Identify all three features.", "Choose any two features to write about. For each, write at least one sentence about your own observations or what you find most interesting."],
 closing:"Go back to the Bayeux Tapestry. It is called a tapestry, but it is not one: those pictures are stitched onto cloth that had already been woven. Here the pictures are the cloth. The weavers built them thread by thread on the loom, as the cloth itself was made. Three hundred years apart, two enormous stories told in wool, by two completely different methods. Which of the two do you think would be harder to correct if you made a mistake?",
 bonus:{points:5,prompt:"For 5 bonus points, add any additional observations about the work in general, or question(s) you would like to pursue later.",placeholder:""},
 guide:"Evidence filed. A giant reader, two rows of scenes and backgrounds that switch are features a detective can carry to the next case.",
 sourceNums:[134,135],
 credit:{"word": "Photo", "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:France-001407_-_Apocalypse_Tapestry_(15369796881).jpg", "author": "Dennis G. Jarvis", "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0", "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0/", "changes": "Resized from the original."}};
